{% doc %}
  blocks/_featured-pills-tabs.liquid

  The pill chooser at the top of **Featured pills**, and the panels it opens.

  ## It is the theme's tab group, not a second one

  The markup is `snippets/tab-group.liquid` — the rail, the marker, the panel
  area and the whole ARIA contract — and the behaviour is `<tab-group>` in
  `assets/tabs.js`. Both were written generic for exactly this: a third caller
  that needs a different *look* and none of a different implementation.

  So the keyboard walk, the measured marker that slides rather than switches,
  the strip that scrolls instead of wrapping, the drag on a desktop pointer and
  the panel that rises on every change all arrive with the snippet. What this
  file adds is a class — `tabs--pills` — and the settings that paint it.

  ## Why it is private, and why it is not `blocks/tabs.liquid`

  `blocks/tabs.liquid` is the theme's general strip: Plain and Segmented, a
  vertical rail, a divider, a rail width. Its vocabulary is a table of contents
  beside an answer.

  This one is a row of equally weighted facets, each with a mark of its own, and
  its vocabulary is a *track* — an outlined lozenge the options sit inside, a
  rule that travels under the chosen one, and a mark that fills in when its
  option is picked. Adding all of that to the general block would be six
  settings every FAQ in every store has to scroll past to reach the two it uses.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a chooser with nothing to choose
  between is not a block, and the panels it opens are this section's own.

  ## The strip is empty in the markup

  Each nested **Pill** renders a `role="tabpanel"` carrying `data-tab-label`,
  and `<tab-group>` builds the buttons from those labels — cloning the
  `<template data-tab-button>` each panel ships so the mark, the number and the
  flag travel with the option they belong to.

  This is not a shortcut, it is the only shape that works.
  `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` produces one stream of output, and the buttons
  and the panels have to end up in two different containers. No Liquid emits
  nested blocks twice. Either the buttons are generated from the panels, or the
  merchant types every option's name a second time into this block's settings
  and keeps two lists in sync by hand.

  With JavaScript off every panel stays visible and stacks. All of the content
  is reachable; only the switching is missing.

  ## A note on the range settings below

  Shopify validates every `range` for `(max - min) / step <= 101`, and a range
  that fails it does not fail alone: the schema is rejected whole and *every*
  setting in this block reads as undefined, so the first thing that breaks is a
  preset elsewhere reporting an unrelated setting as missing. Both radius
  sliders stop at 50, which is a lozenge at any strip height this block
  produces.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  comment
    Three looks, one strip.

    **Pills** is this section's own: an outlined track with the options at their
    natural widths inside it and a rule travelling under the chosen one.
    **Segmented** and **Plain** are `blocks/tabs.liquid`'s two, offered here so a
    merchant who wants the filled switch or the bare row does not have to swap
    the block out to get it — the classes are that file's, so both look exactly
    as they do everywhere else in the theme.

    All three are horizontal. A track running down the side of a page with the
    options stacked in it is a menu, not a chooser, so the orientation is fixed
    rather than offered and then quietly ignored.
  endcomment
  assign strip_style = s.strip_style | default: 'pills'

  assign classes = 'tabs--block featured-pills__tabs'
  case strip_style
    when 'segmented'
      assign classes = classes | append: ' tabs--segmented'
    when 'pills'
      assign classes = classes | append: ' tabs--pills'
  endcase

  comment
    ---- the marker ----

    A rule, a filled pill, or nothing.

    **Rule** is the drawn design: the chosen option keeps the track's own
    background and gains a line above and below it, which reads as a page
    selected out of a set rather than as a button pressed. It is the default for
    Pills and it is why the marker is still a travelling element — the line
    slides between options, and a line that appears and disappears would be two
    states rather than one control.

    **Fill** is the pill every other strip in the theme draws. **None** hides
    the marker entirely, for a strip whose options say which is chosen with
    their own mark and their own colour.
  endcomment
  assign marker_style = s.marker_style | default: 'rule'
  if strip_style == 'pills'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' tabs--pills-marker-' | append: marker_style
  endif

  comment
    ---- what **Width** actually sizes ----

    The track, not the block.

    `blocks/tabs.liquid` sizes the whole group, which is right there: a vertical
    rail beside a column of prose is one box and it is as wide as the merchant
    says. Here it would be wrong twice over. The group holds the panels as well
    as the strip, so a fit-width group is a *panel* as wide as four option names
    — the composition inside it would be squeezed into a 600px column in the
    middle of a 1400px section, and nothing about the control the merchant
    touched said anything about the panel.

    So the group is always Fill and this setting reaches the track alone: Fit is
    a lozenge as wide as the options in it, Fill stretches it across the section
    with the options sharing the room. Where a fit track sits is **Alignment**
    below, which is the rail's `justify-content`.

    Two properties per answer, not one, and for the reason
    `snippets/group.liquid` sets out at length: `flex` only means anything to a
    flex item, and the track is one — but an `inline-size` is what sizes it if
    the rail is ever not a flex container. They agree, so neither context is a
    special case.
  endcomment
  if s.width == 'fill'
    assign track_flex = '1 1 auto'
    assign track_size = '100%'
  else
    assign track_flex = '0 1 auto'
    assign track_size = 'auto'
  endif

  comment
    Where the track sits when it is narrower than the section, and where the
    panels line up under it. One control, because a track pushed to the left
    with the panels centred under it is two decisions that can disagree.
  endcomment
  case s.alignment
    when 'start'
      assign align_axis = 'start'
    when 'end'
      assign align_axis = 'end'
    else
      assign align_axis = 'center'
  endcase

  comment
    The line around each option. `none` is a zero width rather than
    `border-style: none`, so the buttons keep the same box either way and
    choosing None does not resize the strip.
  endcomment
  assign tab_border_style = s.tab_border_style | default: 'none'
  assign tab_border_width = 0
  if tab_border_style != 'none'
    assign tab_border_width = s.tab_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
  endif

  comment
    The track's own outline, resolved the same way and for the same reason. With
    no colour picked it is `currentColor` faded by `color-mix`, because the
    colour filters cannot take an alpha channel from a keyword — the pattern
    every border in this theme uses.
  endcomment
  assign track_border_style = s.track_border_style | default: 'dashed'
  assign track_border_width = 0
  if track_border_style != 'none'
    assign track_border_width = s.track_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0

    assign track_border_percent = s.track_border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.track_border_color == blank
      assign track_border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: track_border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign track_border_alpha = track_border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign track_border_paint = s.track_border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', track_border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign gap = s.gap | default: 32 | plus: 0
  assign gap_mobile = s.gap_mobile | default: gap | plus: 0
  assign tab_gap = s.tab_gap | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign tabs_id = 'FeaturedPills-' | append: block.id
-%}

{%- capture tabs_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --tabs-gap: {{ gap }}px;
  --tabs-gap-mobile: {{ gap_mobile }}px;
  --tabs-tab-gap: {{ tab_gap }}px;
  --tabs-rail-justify: {{ align_axis }};
  --tabs-marker-radius: {{ s.marker_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --tabs-track-padding: {{ s.track_padding | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --tabs-track-radius: {{ s.track_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --tabs-track-border-width: {{ track_border_width }}px;
  {%- if track_border_width > 0 -%}
    --tabs-track-border-style: {{ track_border_style }};
    --tabs-track-border-color: {{ track_border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.track_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-track-background: {{ s.track_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.marker_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-marker-background: {{ s.marker_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.marker_rule_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-marker-rule-color: {{ s.marker_rule_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --tabs-marker-rule-width: {{ s.marker_rule_width | default: 2 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --tabs-tab-border-width: {{ tab_border_width }}px;
  {%- if tab_border_width > 0 -%}
    --tabs-tab-border-style: {{ tab_border_style }};
    {%- if s.tab_border_color != blank -%}
      --tabs-tab-border-color: {{ s.tab_border_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  --tabs-tab-padding-block: {{ s.tab_padding_block | default: 10 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --tabs-tab-padding-inline: {{ s.tab_padding_inline | default: 20 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the option's own surface ----

    A track with a background and options with none is the main strip: one
    lozenge, the options reading as regions of it. A track with *no* background
    and options that each carry one is the other arrangement the design uses —
    the numbered routine chooser, which is three white pills on the section's
    own colour with nothing drawn around them.

    Both are the same component with the paint moved, so this is a setting and
    not a second strip style. Left empty it emits nothing and the option is
    transparent, which is what every strip in the theme did before it existed.

    The chosen option takes the second colour when one is given and keeps the
    first when it is not — a strip whose selection is said by a rule or a mark
    does not need its pill to change as well.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.tab_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-background: {{ s.tab_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_background_active != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-background-active: {{ s.tab_background_active }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the two tab colours ----

    Two fields, because they are two decisions: the colour an option's name is,
    and the colour the chosen one becomes.

    **Tab color** has no default: left alone it emits nothing, the name inherits
    the surrounding text colour, and the strip renders against whatever colour
    scheme it landed in. **Selected tab text color** likewise, because on this
    strip the chosen option keeps the track's background — there is no filled
    marker behind it to guarantee a contrast against, so guessing white would be
    wrong on every light track.

    Setting the first switches the dimming off. A merchant who has said what
    colour an unselected name is has already answered the question the opacity
    was guessing at, and leaving it on would serve them a paler version of the
    colour they picked.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.tab_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-color: {{ s.tab_color }};
    --tabs-tab-idle-opacity: 1;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_active_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-active-color: {{ s.tab_active_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    What the chosen option's mark becomes. Emitted here rather than on each
    panel because "the selected one is filled green" is a property of the strip:
    a merchant setting it four times, once per option, is four places for it to
    drift out of step.

    Each is read by `.tabs--pills .tabs__tab[aria-selected='true'] .icon-mark`
    in `assets/header.css` as an override of the `--icon-mark-*` properties the
    panel's own mark published, so an option that never chose a Shape is
    untouched and keeps drawing the glyph on its own.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.mark_active_fill != blank -%}
    --tabs-mark-active-fill: {{ s.mark_active_fill }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.mark_active_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-mark-active-color: {{ s.mark_active_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.mark_check_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-mark-check-background: {{ s.mark_check_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.mark_check_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-mark-check-color: {{ s.mark_check_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Whether the chosen option keeps its ring.

    A property rather than a class because the answer is an opacity and CSS has
    no way to ask "did the merchant pick a fill?" — which is the question this
    is standing in for. The drawn design fills the chosen mark in solid and the
    dotted ring around it has nothing left to do, so Off is the default; a strip
    whose marks are rings rather than discs turns it on and keeps them.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.mark_active_ring -%}
    --tabs-mark-active-ring-opacity: 1;
  {%- else -%}
    --tabs-mark-active-ring-opacity: 0;
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The group fills the section; the track is what **Width** sizes. See the note
    above on why those are two different boxes.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --tabs-flex: 1 1 0%;
  --tabs-width: 100%;
  --tabs-track-flex: {{ track_flex }};
  --tabs-track-width: {{ track_size }};
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Captured, because the shell has to wrap it. `blocks/tabs.liquid` and
  `blocks/section-header.liquid` hand their children on the same way: a block
  stream is a string once it is captured, and a snippet can put a string
  anywhere.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture panels -%}
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture tab_attributes -%}
  data-block-spacing {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The animation wraps the strip rather than replacing it: `<tab-group>` has to
  be the custom element `assets/tabs.js` defines, so the one that animates goes
  around it and takes the width with it — a wrapper that did not would leave the
  strip sized to its content inside a full-width box.

  Its two targets are the rail and the panels, so **Stagger between items** means
  the options arrive and then what they open.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  The no-JS escape hatch for the rule in `assets/header.css` that shows only the
  selected panel until `<tab-group>` upgrades.

  That rule exists because four full compositions stacked is not a fallback, it
  is a wall — and the theme editor replays it on every re-render. But it is
  keyed on `:not(:defined)`, which never stops matching if the module never
  arrives, and then three quarters of the section is unreachable.

  `<noscript>` is rendered only when scripting is off, which is exactly the case
  where the upgrade will not happen. The declaration is unlayered, and an
  unlayered rule beats every `@layer` regardless of specificity or order, so it
  needs no `!important` and no selector arms race with the stylesheet.
{%- endcomment -%}
<noscript>
  <style>
    tab-group.featured-pills__tabs:not(:defined) .tabs__panel { display: block; }
  </style>
</noscript>

<motion-effect
  class="tabs-motion featured-pills__tabs-motion"
  style="--tabs-flex: 1 1 0%; --tabs-width: 100%;"
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s, target: '.tabs__rail, .tabs__panels' %}
>
  {% render 'tab-group',
     id: tabs_id,
     panels: panels,
     orientation: 'horizontal',
     classes: classes,
     label: s.label,
     styles: tabs_style,
     attributes: tab_attributes %}
</motion-effect>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% # theme-check-disable ExcessiveSettingsCount %}
{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "_featured-pills-tab" }
  ],
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.paragraphs.blocks" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.group.headers.layout" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "strip_style",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.strip_style.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "pills", "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.strip_style.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "segmented", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "plain", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.options__1.label" }
      ],
      "default": "pills"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "width",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "fit", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "fill", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "fit"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "alignment",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.alignment.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "start", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "center", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "end", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__3.label" }
      ],
      "default": "center"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.gap.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.gap.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_mobile",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.gap_mobile.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.gap_mobile.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 20
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "tab_gap",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_gap.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_gap.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.label.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.label.info"
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.headers.track" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.paragraphs.track" },
    {
      "type": "color_background",
      "id": "track_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_background.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "track_border_style",
      "label": "t:shared.border.style.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.options.none" },
        { "value": "solid", "label": "t:shared.options.solid" },
        { "value": "dashed", "label": "t:shared.options.dashed" }
      ],
      "default": "dashed"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "track_border_width",
      "label": "t:shared.border.thickness.label",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 6,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 1,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.track_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "track_border_opacity",
      "label": "t:shared.border.opacity.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 40,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.track_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "track_border_color",
      "label": "t:shared.border.color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.track_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "track_padding",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_padding.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 24,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 8
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "track_radius",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_radius.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_radius.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 50,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 50
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.headers.marker" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "marker_style",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.marker_style.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.marker_style.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "rule", "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.marker_style.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "fill", "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.marker_style.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.options.none" }
      ],
      "default": "rule",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style == 'pills' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "marker_rule_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.marker_rule_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style == 'pills' and block.settings.marker_style == 'rule' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "marker_rule_width",
      "label": "t:shared.border.thickness.label",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 8,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 2,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style == 'pills' and block.settings.marker_style == 'rule' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_background",
      "id": "marker_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.marker_background.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style != 'pills' or block.settings.marker_style == 'fill' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "marker_radius",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.marker_radius.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.marker_radius.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 50,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style != 'pills' or block.settings.marker_style == 'fill' }}"
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:shared.appearance.header" },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "tab_padding_block",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_padding_block.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 40,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 10
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "tab_padding_inline",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_padding_inline.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 60,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 20
    },
    {
      "type": "color_background",
      "id": "tab_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_background.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_background.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_background",
      "id": "tab_background_active",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_background_active.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_background_active.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_color.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_active_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_active_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tabs.settings.tab_active_color.info"
    },
    {
